          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Honors and Global Recognition</strong><br>\nSabin received numerous accolades for his work, including the National Medal of Science in 1970. He was widely recognized for his contributions to virology and immunology.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Humanitarian Efforts and Legacy</strong><br>\nThroughout his career, Sabin was committed to humanitarian efforts, working to ensure that vaccines were available to all, regardless of their ability to pay. His dedication to eradicating polio has left a lasting impact on global health.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Continuing Impact</strong><br>\nAlbert Sabin died on March 3, 1993. His oral vaccine continues to play a critical role in the fight against polio, with his legacy carried on through ongoing efforts to eliminate the disease worldwide.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
